<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint-Domingue</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint-Domingue was the French colony on the western part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. In the eighteenth century its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantations, worked by enslaved Africans, made it one of the most profitable colonies in the world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> that began in 1791 ended French rule, and the colony became the independent state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in 1804, a major loss for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-domingue.jpg" alt="Saint-Domingue" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bluebear2 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pavillon_LouisXIV.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1492. In 1605 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-spain">Philip III of Spain</a> ordered Governor Antonio de Osorio to depopulate the western, central and northern parts of the island to stop smuggling with French, English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> traders. French, English and Dutch pirates instead set up bases on the abandoned coasts. French buccaneers settled on Tortuga in 1625, and the settlement was officially established in 1659 under a commission from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>.</p><p>Plantation agriculture based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> began in 1665. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-dogeron">Bertrand d'Ogeron</a> encouraged tobacco planting, and after a tobacco crisis around 1670 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> and cane <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> were introduced; the first sugar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmill</a> was built in 1685. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> tacitly recognised the French presence in the western third of the island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ryswick">Peace of Ryswick</a> of 1697.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> the colony expanded rapidly. By the 1780s it produced about 40 percent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and 60 percent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> consumed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, from about 8,000 plantations by 1789. Between 1681 and 1791 an estimated 790,000 or 860,000 enslaved people provided the labour, a third of the entire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> in 1783 to 1791. Disease and harsh conditions prevented natural increase, so most of the enslaved were African-born.</p><p>Estimates for 1789 give 40,000 to 45,000 whites, 28,000 to 32,000 free people of color and between 406,000 and 465,000 enslaved people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gens-de-couleur-libres">gens de couleur libres</a> were the largest and wealthiest such group in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> was at first welcomed on the island. In 1791 an uprising of the enslaved in the north burned 280 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations within two months. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> took charge of the revolt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished in 1793. The last French troops left the western part of the island in late 1803, and the colony declared independence the following year as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. More than 25,000 colonists fled between 1791 and 1810, many to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>.</p><p>On 17 April 1825 Charles X issued a decree recognising Haitian independence in return for 150 million francs, to compensate former colonists. On 11 July 1825 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-pierre-boyer">Jean-Pierre Boyer</a> signed, with a French naval squadron present. Researchers have linked the resulting debt to later underfunding of education, health care and public works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint-Domingue” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 1780s <b>Saint-Domingue</b>, now Haiti, produced around 40 per cent of Europe's sugar and some 60 per cent of its coffee.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century Bordeaux and Nantes grew rich on Atlantic trade in sugar, coffee and enslaved Africans, centred on the colony of <b>Saint-Domingue</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Haiti, the Taíno name for Hispaniola, was reclaimed in 1804 by Jean-Jacques Dessalines for the state the enslaved people of French <b>Saint-Domingue</b> had won.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain ceded western Hispaniola to France in 1697, and in the 18th century the plantation colony of <b>Saint-Domingue</b> imported enslaved Africans in great numbers.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In August that year the enslaved of the Northern Plain of <b>Saint-Domingue</b>, France’s richest colony, rose in revolt.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The first wave of colonial independence came in the Americas: the thirteen British colonies declared independence in 1776, the enslaved of <b>Saint-Domingue</b> made Haiti independent in 1804, and Spain lost its mainland American empire in the wars of 1808–1833.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
